1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,763
A valuation cap and a discount
are two different ways a SAFE can

2
00:00:03,763 --> 00:00:07,216
set a conversion price, if its terms
include them.

3
00:00:07,216 --> 00:00:10,941
A cap uses a stated company
valuation and a capitalization

4
00:00:10,941 --> 00:00:15,631
definition; a discount reduces the
next financing’s share price by a

5
00:00:15,631 --> 00:00:16,856
percentage.

6
00:00:16,856 --> 00:00:22,616
In a simplified example, assume a
$500 SAFE, an $8 million cap, a

7
00:00:22,616 --> 00:00:26,534
$20 million priced round, and ten
million fully diluted

8
00:00:26,534 --> 00:00:28,312
pre-conversion shares.

9
00:00:28,312 --> 00:00:32,410
The cap calculation gives $0.80
per share, while the round price is

10
00:00:32,410 --> 00:00:36,985
$2.00, so the cap-based
illustration gives 625 shares

11
00:00:36,985 --> 00:00:39,807
versus 250 at the round price.

12
00:00:39,807 --> 00:00:42,900
That is not a guaranteed
ownership percentage.

13
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:46,224
It ignores new money, other
instruments, option-pool

14
00:00:46,224 --> 00:00:49,291
changes, and contract-specific
details.

15
00:00:49,291 --> 00:00:53,827
If a SAFE also has a 20% discount
to a $2 round price, the

16
00:00:53,827 --> 00:00:57,345
discounted price would be $1.60.

17
00:00:57,345 --> 00:01:00,644
Which term governs depends on
the actual agreement.

18
00:01:00,644 --> 00:01:04,111
Check the capitalization
definition and conversion formula

19
00:01:04,111 --> 00:01:05,160
carefully.

